1
00:00:01,910 --> 00:00:27,750
Let's turn, what is the future, is government going to go away?

2
00:00:28,050 --> 00:00:39,230
The answer is, it may become a little more competent than God knows we need it.

3
00:00:39,870 --> 00:00:47,160
It may continue as a long slide towards incompetence, but unfortunately it ain't going to go away.

4
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:52,460
So accept the fact that there is a government in your future.

5
00:00:52,460 --> 00:02:51,600
Well, I usually divide questions in such a conference as ours, such as the symposium,

6
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:58,000
into three groups. There are the questions where the question never changes and the answers do,

7
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:04,400
and the ones where the answers never change but the questions do. And in between there are those

8
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:09,920
where the questions never change and the answers don't either. And I would have ten years ago,

9
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:13,440
or even five years ago, put this question into this last group.

10
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:17,120
It's an old question, and the answers seem never to have changed.

11
00:03:17,660 --> 00:03:21,080
And fundamentally, the answer was, and in a way, Bill has given it,

12
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:25,480
is that managers don't do anything too different today

13
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:27,840
from what they did 70 years ago.

14
00:03:27,980 --> 00:03:31,160
We only have so many more of them that they have to learn it.

15
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:36,420
And I am beginning to wonder whether this question is not moving

16
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:39,480
to where the question doesn't change and the answers do.

17
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:42,340
But, of course, there are some new things.

18
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:47,980
Number one, your last question about government regulation.

19
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:49,940
I'm going to tell you a secret.

20
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:52,680
The manager, 10 years hence, will not ask it

21
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:59,240
because he will have learned that there are multiple constituencies.

22
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:04,940
Today, particularly the business manager, still believes, and it was true,

23
00:04:05,740 --> 00:04:09,800
that as long as he produces a decent product and service,

24
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:11,600
sells it at a profit.

25
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:14,660
The other constituencies are restraints,

26
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:19,690
whether they are employees or the managers afterwards,

27
00:04:19,830 --> 00:04:22,170
but to look at stockholders as foreigners,

28
00:04:23,090 --> 00:04:26,450
or whether they are the government, so on.

29
00:04:26,910 --> 00:04:31,550
And they are beginning to be what any public institution service manager

30
00:04:31,550 --> 00:04:34,090
has always known, they are constituencies,

31
00:04:34,590 --> 00:04:37,170
which means any one of them can veto you,

32
00:04:39,130 --> 00:04:42,330
And yet not one of them is concerned with your success.

33
00:04:44,380 --> 00:04:52,800
And so constituency management, the management of the other institutions in the environment,

34
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:59,380
the ecology, if you want to call it that, of this jungle, yes, they will have to learn it.

35
00:04:59,380 --> 00:05:11,160
And another thing, one can predict with confidence that the present business school's weakest point

36
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:16,900
is going to become a tremendous defect.

37
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:21,300
That is the absence of any real understanding of production management.

38
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:24,740
We once taught it 50 years ago.

39
00:05:25,380 --> 00:05:27,280
It was a miserable discipline.

40
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:30,960
A hodgepodge of how many toilets per hundred employees.

41
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:35,340
You know, look, the first American textbook of management

42
00:05:35,340 --> 00:05:39,700
written by a professor at NYU, his name was Cornell, in 1916,

43
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:42,000
was still being taught in 1940.

44
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:47,220
is an assembly of trivial miscellaneous,

45
00:05:48,330 --> 00:05:50,750
from alphabetically organized,

46
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:55,010
from, because it has no,

47
00:05:55,090 --> 00:05:57,550
there is no discipline of production management.

48
00:05:57,730 --> 00:05:58,610
There still isn't one.

49
00:05:59,130 --> 00:06:02,130
And so for good intellectual reasons,

50
00:06:02,230 --> 00:06:03,570
we have stopped teaching it.

51
00:06:05,070 --> 00:06:07,250
If only because the younger ones,

52
00:06:07,250 --> 00:06:09,570
because I refuse to teach that nonsense.

53
00:06:10,700 --> 00:06:14,710
We will not be able to do it

54
00:06:14,710 --> 00:06:22,370
because the great challenge, the microprocessor is a genuine revolution

55
00:06:22,370 --> 00:06:25,330
in the way we produce things,

56
00:06:26,070 --> 00:06:30,920
where we organize the process of making around information,

57
00:06:31,400 --> 00:06:34,780
where the last one organized it around materials transport.

58
00:06:36,340 --> 00:06:38,520
And we need production management.

59
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:44,760
And believe me, the MBAs don't know what it is, and they're hopeless,

60
00:06:44,980 --> 00:06:47,520
and the engineers don't know what it is.

61
00:06:47,520 --> 00:07:08,280
And so you can say 10 years hence, we will have to have people who know what it is and who just don't go about it the way we do today, where it's a no-man's land between engineers and accountants, with a personal man in between.

62
00:07:08,280 --> 00:07:30,520
And the third thing one can probably predict is that the manager of tomorrow has to learn to take responsibility for his effectiveness, his communications, his relationship, his career, and his placement.

63
00:07:30,520 --> 00:07:50,020
And today the manager expects the great white father on the 14th floor to do that. And it's much too complex. It's much, above all, the diversity is so great of career paths and changing them.

64
00:07:50,020 --> 00:07:52,520
As Bill said, you have to be yourself.

65
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:59,340
There is the idea that you can adopt a style that doesn't fit your personality.

66
00:08:02,690 --> 00:08:08,990
I once had a very good friend who was, by way of being a Shakespeare scholar,

67
00:08:09,210 --> 00:08:13,070
he said the one play he couldn't accept was The Merchant of Venice

68
00:08:13,070 --> 00:08:17,170
because it is absolutely unthinkable that even a judge,

69
00:08:17,210 --> 00:08:19,770
he was one and had a low opinion of the profession,

70
00:08:19,770 --> 00:08:25,130
that even a judge could for one minute be fooled by Portia's false beard.

71
00:08:25,730 --> 00:08:29,350
There was a woman, obviously, but it's the most female character in Shakespeare.

72
00:08:30,150 --> 00:08:36,050
One can't fool even a judge by a false beard for more than a minute, except in a play.

73
00:08:36,410 --> 00:08:37,990
One has to be oneself.

74
00:08:38,910 --> 00:08:41,930
There's no foreign style you can adapt.

75
00:08:43,450 --> 00:08:45,790
And so you will have to manage of tomorrow.

76
00:08:45,790 --> 00:08:52,230
I would have to take, again, a degree of responsibility for where do I belong, where do I go,

77
00:08:54,850 --> 00:09:02,370
which basically the last 30 years, or even 50 years, we have not stressed.

78
00:09:02,950 --> 00:09:08,690
So there are new things, but when we need, I think production management,

79
00:09:08,690 --> 00:09:15,170
if any young man came to me, a woman, and said, I want to go into business, what do I learn?

80
00:09:15,170 --> 00:09:18,790
And I said, this is the time for you to go into the plant.

81
00:09:19,760 --> 00:09:22,740
Stay out of the accounting office if you're a young man.

82
00:09:22,980 --> 00:09:24,640
You can't compete with the women.

83
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:25,640
You aren't bright enough.

84
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:28,520
And they're all in there.

85
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:32,820
But the plant, the women haven't discovered it yet.

86
00:09:33,340 --> 00:09:37,300
And learn plant management, production management,

87
00:09:37,700 --> 00:09:39,260
because nobody knows it.

88
00:09:39,260 --> 00:09:42,680
And it's going to be badly needed.

89
00:09:42,680 --> 00:09:52,220
and I would say learn how one finds out that politicians behave like politicians

90
00:09:52,220 --> 00:09:59,120
and what does that mean and how other groups see the world

91
00:09:59,120 --> 00:10:03,510
that you can think through what does the expector mean,

92
00:10:03,690 --> 00:10:06,680
how can we build that into performance

93
00:10:06,680 --> 00:10:12,810
and finally learn to ask the question who am I, what do I do well,

94
00:10:13,350 --> 00:10:16,570
how do I build back feedback from my performance

95
00:10:16,570 --> 00:10:23,090
so that I improve myself and learn what I can do and what I can't do.

96
00:10:23,470 --> 00:10:26,070
And how do I decide where I really belong?

97
00:10:27,290 --> 00:10:33,780
Those are the three things I would say are the new answers to an old question.

98
00:10:33,780 --> 00:11:01,850
Dean Taggart, can you hear me?

99
00:11:03,140 --> 00:11:07,440
May we please say that our dearly beloved dean is here.

100
00:11:07,540 --> 00:11:10,280
Will you stand up, Joe, that we can applaud you?

101
00:11:10,280 --> 00:11:25,380
This building and this school is very largely a living monument to him, and I'm deeply grateful

102
00:11:25,380 --> 00:11:27,840
that you came here, Joe, today.

103
00:11:27,840 --> 00:11:28,840
Thanks.

104
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:36,260
You know, I'm being asked, I always wonder about the question people don't ask.

105
00:11:36,260 --> 00:11:42,960
And the question about the present administration that people don't ask is, why does everybody

106
00:11:42,960 --> 00:11:46,820
think it makes so much of a difference?

107
00:11:46,820 --> 00:11:56,310
When you look at the election, Mr. Reagan won decisively, but hardly, an enormous landslide.

108
00:11:56,310 --> 00:12:01,670
And let's put it that way, Mr. Carter lost more than Mr. Reagan won.

109
00:12:01,670 --> 00:12:08,800
And when you look at the last few years of the Carter administration, all the things

110
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:17,460
Mr. Reagan proposed, Mr. Carter was at least talking about, shift towards defense, realization

111
00:12:17,460 --> 00:12:23,540
that maybe the world isn't quite as harmless as the taunt assumed it to be,

112
00:12:23,540 --> 00:12:33,310
and maybe the realization that the more money you move from the producers to the non-producers,

113
00:12:34,270 --> 00:12:37,450
the less will be left over for productive purposes.

114
00:12:38,250 --> 00:12:43,810
What else is new? Why makes it such a difference?

115
00:12:43,810 --> 00:13:13,460
And one clue, by the way, maybe the most important thing that has happened since the 20th of January, is the new Secretary of Defense's public statement in the ATT antitrust suit, which said that breaking up the Bell system would be an exceedingly detrimental thing to do from the point of view of national defense,

116
00:13:13,460 --> 00:13:17,360
which breaks a very old American taboo,

117
00:13:17,560 --> 00:13:22,980
which is a taboo against thinking about anything but a peacetime society

118
00:13:22,980 --> 00:13:25,160
until the day war breaks out.

119
00:13:26,190 --> 00:13:27,170
It's an old taboo.

120
00:13:27,750 --> 00:13:30,510
It goes back to, if you want, Jefferson.

121
00:13:31,380 --> 00:13:34,200
And he broke it, and nobody thought it extraordinary.

122
00:13:34,980 --> 00:13:40,080
Mind you, those of us who know a little bit about the country's defense needs

123
00:13:40,080 --> 00:13:43,040
asked that question all along,

124
00:13:43,040 --> 00:13:45,340
but nobody there would say it

125
00:13:45,340 --> 00:13:47,480
and whether the antitrust

126
00:13:47,480 --> 00:13:49,320
department and the court hear it

127
00:13:49,320 --> 00:13:51,360
I don't know yet and by the

128
00:13:51,360 --> 00:13:55,020
way don't be surprised

129
00:13:55,020 --> 00:13:56,600
if in a few weeks from now

130
00:13:56,600 --> 00:13:59,000
somebody there in Washington will say

131
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,200
what will it do to American

132
00:14:01,200 --> 00:14:03,060
exports if we break up IBM

133
00:14:03,060 --> 00:14:06,290
it's

134
00:14:06,290 --> 00:14:08,610
our largest next to GE

135
00:14:08,610 --> 00:14:10,490
our largest single exporter

136
00:14:10,490 --> 00:14:11,790
and others in agriculture

137
00:14:11,790 --> 00:14:14,490
and maybe one

138
00:14:14,490 --> 00:14:22,800
doesn't take chances. And that would also be unspeakable because the idea that what

139
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:31,020
happens offshore has any impact on domestic morality and verities is also unspeakable.

140
00:14:31,320 --> 00:14:40,530
So something has happened. And perhaps the thing that is most significant is that despite

141
00:14:40,530 --> 00:14:49,400
the supply-side economists. We all realize that we have lost the, make it almost 50 years, in which

142
00:14:49,400 --> 00:14:57,850
we had a working economic theory. And they came there and say no, and the Friedmanites say no,

143
00:14:57,970 --> 00:15:04,130
and the supply-siders say we have one. But I don't think anybody believes them anymore. By the way,

144
00:15:04,210 --> 00:15:11,150
I don't think the supply-siders believe themselves. For the simple reason that we know that the

145
00:15:11,150 --> 00:15:19,160
assumptions have become dubious and we are in the throes of a genuine scientific revolution in which

146
00:15:19,160 --> 00:15:28,890
the paradigms no longer hold and it's the sixth as Tom mentioned since economics became a discipline

147
00:15:28,890 --> 00:15:36,810
with the assertion which was a brand new heresy at the end of the 16th century that there is an

148
00:15:36,810 --> 00:15:44,440
autonomous economic realm governments have always worried about money and taxes and trade and

149
00:15:44,440 --> 00:15:49,860
individuals have always worried about incomes but economics as a separate discipline is their

150
00:15:49,860 --> 00:15:57,360
modern heresy and was very heretical when it first came up and when you look at it the last one

151
00:15:57,360 --> 00:16:05,360
the Keynesian one and I'm old enough to have been the youngest invited member of the Keynes seminar

152
00:16:05,360 --> 00:16:18,070
and Cambridge in the early 30s did three things of which two were brand new and one was a return

153
00:16:18,790 --> 00:16:27,350
to an old and once discredited basic assumption the discredited one was the macroeconomic one

154
00:16:28,960 --> 00:16:37,060
we went off economics started as macroeconomics and it was such a fiasco the mercantilists

155
00:16:37,060 --> 00:16:41,280
In the same areas in which our present macroeconomics are a fiasco,

156
00:16:41,900 --> 00:16:44,160
productivity and capital formation,

157
00:16:44,940 --> 00:16:49,280
they ruined the richest country of Europe in 50 years, France.

158
00:16:50,820 --> 00:16:56,510
And the more of a surplus they had, the poorer they were.

159
00:16:58,020 --> 00:17:02,560
And the less productive and the more did capital formation fall.

160
00:17:02,680 --> 00:17:06,540
And so the first scientific revolution that of the physiocrats

161
00:17:06,540 --> 00:17:11,400
went microeconomic by looking at the English who had no economic theory

162
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:13,440
and therefore did exceedingly well.

163
00:17:14,100 --> 00:17:19,260
And let me say, before 1780, there were no economists in England.

164
00:17:19,900 --> 00:17:22,060
It's one of the secrets of England's rise.

165
00:17:23,900 --> 00:17:31,330
And people did what worked and didn't ask why.

166
00:17:32,810 --> 00:17:43,200
And microeconomic, in the 1930s, we went back to macroeconomic.

167
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:52,160
And the results are the same. I think one can now say, you know, there are two experiments

168
00:17:52,160 --> 00:17:59,460
prove that kind, or disprove a theorem. The purely macroeconomic system does not, cannot

169
00:17:59,460 --> 00:18:06,950
assume, as did both mercantilists and the Keynesians, and never really became one, but

170
00:18:06,950 --> 00:18:14,150
that was close enough, that if only the macroeconomy is in good shape, the microeconomy will take

171
00:18:14,150 --> 00:18:22,190
care of itself. The basic Keynesian assumption, which the supply-siders share, and the Friedmanites

172
00:18:22,190 --> 00:18:30,710
share, that the microeconomy is determined, is like an ideal gas, while individual molecules

173
00:18:30,710 --> 00:18:37,390
may have Brownian motions, the whole thing moves statistically. Isn't true. The individual

174
00:18:37,390 --> 00:18:46,060
molecules have their own dynamics they are not determined and that once and for all basically

175
00:18:46,060 --> 00:18:54,580
makes obsolete any economic theory we have today because we have none other but the two other

176
00:18:54,580 --> 00:19:00,900
Keynesian assertions which were equally were more novel believe me Keynes was quite right when he

177
00:19:00,900 --> 00:19:08,620
said of himself that in terms of economic theory was a much greater innovator than Marx who just

178
00:19:08,620 --> 00:19:16,180
kept that part of traditional classical economics that suited him and disregarded the rest.

179
00:19:18,100 --> 00:19:25,620
And Keynes actually did two other things. He first switched economics to where it had never been,

180
00:19:25,620 --> 00:19:36,610
namely to demand. Before Keynes, every economics we had was a supply-side economics. Demand was

181
00:19:36,610 --> 00:19:43,810
Petro definition unlimited, supply was the problem. And Keynes stood it on its head for

182
00:19:43,810 --> 00:19:50,370
good reasons at the time, by the way. And it's now clear that it is just as untrue.

183
00:19:52,130 --> 00:20:00,000
To say that, as Say never said, Keynes might say that Say's law was Keynes' invention,

184
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:04,000
as you probably know. In all the books there is no mention of it.

185
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:17,360
it. But Keynes' investment being a totally dependent function of demand, which is a multiplier

186
00:20:17,360 --> 00:20:23,440
translating consumption into investment and therefore into jobs, has been totally disproven.

187
00:20:23,440 --> 00:20:29,510
In fact, it never worked. It didn't work in the 30s, and it hasn't worked since in any

188
00:20:29,510 --> 00:20:38,870
country there is and so the opposite one that investment as a function of savings and therefore

189
00:20:38,870 --> 00:20:49,270
of supply which says law never quite said also didn't work we have no truly dependent function

190
00:20:49,270 --> 00:20:58,930
except the one that jobs are a function of investment but we have no function no invest

191
00:20:58,930 --> 00:21:07,720
no function for investment the savings function is more new works let me say says law works more

192
00:21:07,720 --> 00:21:13,160
much better than kane's law does but it doesn't work well enough and kane's law doesn't work at

193
00:21:13,160 --> 00:21:22,660
all and we have no predictor in there except that investment creates jobs anybody interested in it

194
00:21:22,660 --> 00:21:27,540
i have an editorial in today's wall street journal called the full investment budget

195
00:21:27,540 --> 00:21:33,620
in which I talk about the fact that the full employment budget which made Keynesian economics

196
00:21:33,620 --> 00:21:40,900
politically operational, Walter Heller's invention of it in 1957 that then led to the Kennedy

197
00:21:40,900 --> 00:21:48,100
economics, that has been disproven. But we now need a full investment budget to have full

198
00:21:48,100 --> 00:21:54,100
employment that looks at the number of jobs we have to create and the capital investment needed

199
00:21:54,100 --> 00:21:57,640
for them, and then looks at, is there enough capital formation for it?

200
00:21:57,960 --> 00:22:02,120
And we can't depend on any indirect dependent function

201
00:22:02,120 --> 00:22:04,770
for the investment function anymore.

202
00:22:05,470 --> 00:22:10,670
But that was central to Keynes, as it is central to Friedman

203
00:22:10,670 --> 00:22:14,970
and amazingly enough central to the supply-side people.

204
00:22:14,970 --> 00:22:17,170
And it has been disproven.

205
00:22:17,910 --> 00:22:23,270
And the third Keynesian one was that Keynes solved

206
00:22:23,270 --> 00:22:36,780
The oldest problem of economics, that between the real economy and the symbol economy, the real economy of goods and services, the symbol economy of money and credit.

207
00:22:37,420 --> 00:22:47,140
The predecessors up till 1870 had looked upon money as being the veil of reality, not reality itself.

208
00:22:47,140 --> 00:22:51,340
and the events that matter happen in the real economy.

209
00:22:52,400 --> 00:22:55,800
That was doubted very early by people like McCullough.

210
00:22:56,420 --> 00:23:01,060
And in 1870, the Austrians said,

211
00:23:01,160 --> 00:23:05,720
this doesn't work at all, we don't play that game.

212
00:23:06,400 --> 00:23:07,140
And it didn't.

213
00:23:07,260 --> 00:23:10,480
One of the main reasons why the traditional Austrian economics

214
00:23:10,480 --> 00:23:12,400
became intellectually untenable

215
00:23:12,400 --> 00:23:18,390
was this refusal of the Austrians to tackle the crucial problem.

216
00:23:18,390 --> 00:23:21,470
This is very much like a physician saying,

217
00:23:21,970 --> 00:23:28,230
I will learn anatomy, but I will not include the heart in it.

218
00:23:28,310 --> 00:23:29,270
It's too complicated.

219
00:23:29,990 --> 00:23:34,610
That is a basic weakness, intellectual flaw of Austrian economics,

220
00:23:35,050 --> 00:23:37,990
which is why we will not return to them,

221
00:23:38,130 --> 00:23:41,010
no matter how attractive they may be politically.

222
00:23:42,590 --> 00:23:47,290
They get their rigor by excluding the central problem.

223
00:23:47,610 --> 00:23:49,550
That's no way of doing it.

224
00:23:49,550 --> 00:23:54,010
The central problem is the relationship between those two.

225
00:23:55,330 --> 00:23:58,790
And Keynes solved it by saying there ain't no goods and services.

226
00:23:59,850 --> 00:24:02,450
There's only money and only simple economics.

227
